Challenge: Find one AI use case that you think is surprisingly useful and explain why.\par
 AI-Based Electricity Saving System\par
The AI-Based Electricity Saving System helps people save electricity in homes, colleges, offices, and businesses. It collects information about the electricity used by lights, fans, ACs, and computers.AI checks this information and learns the normal usage. If too much electricity is being used or some appliances are not needed, the system alerts the user and gives suggestions to save electricity.For example, it may suggest  turning off unused appliances or using the AC less during some hours. It can also predict future electricity usage and help reduce electricity bills.\par
This system helps save electricity, save money, and reduce energy waste\par
\par

\pard\keep\keepn\sb480\sl276\slmult1\cf1\b\f2\fs28 7. Step 4 \f1\endash\f2  Mini Practical Exercise: Design an AI Solution\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0\b0\f0\fs21 Choose ONE problem from your college, personal life, local community, or a business you know. Design a simple AI-based solution. You do not need to build the software.\par
1. What problem are you trying to solve?\par
A.I am trying to solve the problem of unnecessary electricity usage and energy waste\par
2. Who faces this problem?\par
A.Students, families, offices, colleges, and businesses face this problem\par
3. What information/data would your system need?\par
A.The system would need data about electricity usage from lights, fans, ACs, computers, and other appliances.\par
4. Would you use AI, ML, DL, GenAI, or a combination? Why?\par
A.I would use Machine Learning (ML) because it can learn the normal electricity usage and find unusual usage.\par
5. What would the system take as input?\par
A.It would take electricity usage data and the time of usage.\par
6. What would the system produce as output?\par
A.It would give alerts, saving suggestions, and future electricity usage predictions\par
7. Give one example of how a user would interact with it.\par
A.For example, a user checks the app and sees an alert saying that the AC is using too much electricity\par
8. What could go wrong with your solution?\par
A. The system may give a wrong alert or make an incorrect prediction about electricity usage.\par
9. How would you reduce or handle that risk?\par
A.I would use accurate data, test the system regularly, and allow users to check the information before taking action.\par
Example idea (do not copy): A college assistant that answers student questions about assignments, attendance rules, and course material.\par

